1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,719
A Zone user asks to withdraw.

2
00:00:02,769 --> 00:00:08,719
Tempo pays it out only from a batch that passed the Zone's verifier,

3
00:00:08,769 --> 00:00:11,950
so Tempo has to trust that check.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,705
This is Sworn's public page, reading Moderato, Tempo's testnet.

5
00:00:16,755 --> 00:00:18,290
Nothing is signed.

6
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:24,629
On Moderato today, the pre-T13 Solidity reference verifier is a prototype stub:

7
00:00:24,679 --> 00:00:27,799
it returns true without checking execution.

8
00:00:27,849 --> 00:00:35,195
I call it live with a malformed batch: zone ninety-nine, config dead, proof beef.

9
00:00:35,245 --> 00:00:36,780
It returns true.

10
00:00:36,830 --> 00:00:39,950
Sworn demonstrates the missing ZK check.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,612
Now Sworn, on a real batch from Tempo's integration tests: two user transactions and one withdrawal.

12
00:00:47,662 --> 00:00:54,795
Tempo's own batch verifier ran inside a zero-knowledge VM, and this contract checked the proof.

13
00:00:54,845 --> 00:00:57,668
Here it is on the explorer.

14
00:00:57,718 --> 00:01:00,542
Again, live: the real batch, true.

15
00:01:00,592 --> 00:01:04,373
Change one field, and it reverts: invalid proof.

16
00:01:04,423 --> 00:01:09,640
An equivalent malformed batch is accepted by Moderato's current prototype verifier,

17
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:13,950
while Sworn rejects a mutation of its proven batch.

18
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,670
To be clear: this batch comes from a dev chain, not a Moderato Zone.

19
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:25,950
No portal uses it, and it does not protect withdrawals yet.

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,009
Separately, the same engine re-runs Tempo's EVM for bonded answers.

21
00:01:31,059 --> 00:01:36,574
A paying agent asks a provider: will the receiver get 500?

22
00:01:36,624 --> 00:01:39,609
This provider is configured to lie.

23
00:01:39,659 --> 00:01:44,668
Before answering, it reserves 500 of its bond as coverage.

24
00:01:44,718 --> 00:01:46,185
The agent pays.

25
00:01:46,235 --> 00:01:52,762
The transaction succeeds, but the receiver gets zero; Tempo's guard holds the money.

26
00:01:52,812 --> 00:01:57,821
Without Sworn, the agent sees success and chases a refund.

27
00:01:57,871 --> 00:02:01,868
Here, a challenger proves it in zero knowledge,

28
00:02:01,918 --> 00:02:06,926
sped up, and the bond pays the agent 500 automatically.

29
00:02:06,976 --> 00:02:08,950
That's compensation, not prevention.

30
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:16,379
The next step is a proposal, spec 004: run a TEE and a ZK proof together,

31
00:02:16,429 --> 00:02:20,557
and hold withdrawals until both have checked the batch.

32
00:02:20,607 --> 00:02:21,950
That needs Tempo.

33
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,057
Today this is testnet and unaudited, with no users or revenue.

34
00:02:27,107 --> 00:02:33,093
The page re-runs these checks from chain, and the code is open source.

35
00:02:33,143 --> 00:02:34,950
Sworn: Tempo's execution, proven.
